WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.440
What is it that makes a person a hero? Is&nbsp;
it a trait that you’re born with; something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:11.600
you can inherit, passed down from generation to&nbsp;
generation? Is someone still a hero if they never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:16.880
save a life, but improve the lives of the people&nbsp;
that matter to them? Do you really need a magic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:22.960
hammer, an enchanted suit of armor, or a tragic&nbsp;
origin story in order to become a hero, or can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:29.360
you still be heroic as an ordinary, everyday&nbsp;
person, through the simplest of kind deeds?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:34.960
Maybe it only takes a few moments in a lifetime to&nbsp;
be a hero, moments when you are given the chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:41.360
to overcome adversity or conquer a personal flaw.
Perhaps learning how to deal with defeat and get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:47.840
back up after, rather than learning how to never&nbsp;
be defeated, is the essence of heroism. We can all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:55.520
be heroes, every single one of us, and in a way,&nbsp;
we already are. Everyone is the hero of their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:03.280
story, and if you want any further proof of that,&nbsp;
then maybe you should take a look inside SCP-1230.&nbsp;

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:09.680
An unassuming book is presented to you, its hard&nbsp;
cover is dark green, but other than that there is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:15.280
absolutely nothing remarkable about it that you&nbsp;
can notice. But little do you know, this book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:22.080
that you hold in your hands is none other than&nbsp;
SCP-1230. So, what’s inside? Surely it must be an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:27.680
important anomalous artifact if the SCP Foundation&nbsp;
has an interest in it. Perhaps it’s filled with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:33.200
ancient spells, or a mathematical formula to&nbsp;
calculate the exact mass of the fabric of reality?&nbsp;

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.360
No, if you’re expecting every page&nbsp;
to be full, you’d be disappointed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:42.160
In fact, only the first page you open in&nbsp;
the book will have anything written on it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:47.680
the rest will appear blank. And this can&nbsp;
change depending on which page you open first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:52.640
resetting every time the book is closed again.&nbsp;
However, the phrase you will find written on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.640 --> 00:02:01.200
that page will always stay the same. When opening&nbsp;
SCP-1230, you will see the words “A hero is born.”&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:06.800
What happens after you read those words? Well,&nbsp;
not much, at least not right away. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:13.280
you won’t notice any active effects after reading&nbsp;
SCP-1230 for quite some time. Until that is, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:20.480
fall asleep, and as you sleep, you begin to dream.
Now, most dreams are pretty disjointed, sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:26.480
fragments of memories or your deepest, most random&nbsp;
thoughts. Of course, other times it’s a fear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:31.040
a long-held and deep-rooted phobia or&nbsp;
manifestation of anxiety brought to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.040 --> 00:02:36.080
surface for a while in the form of a nightmare,&nbsp;
only to be forgotten by the time you’ve woken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.560
up. Perhaps that’s why so many people look&nbsp;
for the deeper meaning to their dreams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:46.640
to get a better understanding of themselves.
But this dream is different, far more vivid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:51.280
and memorable, with a meaning that is&nbsp;
a lot easier to discern. In this dream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:57.520
you find yourself as the hero of your own story,&nbsp;
the protagonist of a fantastical adventure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:04.080
It’s more than an ordinary dream, it looks,&nbsp;
sounds, smells, feels as real as the ordinary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:09.040
world you know. You will find yourself at the&nbsp;
center of a fantasy world, where you are the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:15.520
hero destined to aid this troubled land, to&nbsp;
bring peace, and save the world. The fantasy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:20.000
you find yourself in is different depending&nbsp;
on who you are, it’s a dreamscape that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:26.080
been tailor-made for your enjoyment, and your epic&nbsp;
quest awaits on the path laid out in front of you.&nbsp;

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:32.080
But where do these bespoke fantasy adventures come&nbsp;
from? Does the SCP-1230 book itself bestow these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:37.280
dreams on a reader, or is there some other force&nbsp;
at play that creates worlds where the dreamer is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:45.360
the hero? Well, actually yes, there is, and&nbsp;
his name is The Book Keeper, or SCP-1230-1.&nbsp;

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:50.800
He looks exactly as you would imagine him&nbsp;
to, an old man adorned in a dark green cloak,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:55.200
with a long beard, sometimes appearing&nbsp;
to dreamers in order to guide them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:59.760
Picture Gandalf from the Lord of the Rings&nbsp;
books, but instead of ‘the grey’ or ‘the white’,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:06.560
the Book Keeper is Gandalf the Green. In every&nbsp;
dream caused by SCP-1230, the Book Keeper appears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:12.400
as the personification of the book itself, often&nbsp;
aiding dreamers, willingly offering them counsel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:18.480
guidance, and help on their fantastical quests.
In an experiment to test the range of SCP-1230’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:23.120
anomalous properties, an SCP Foundation&nbsp;
researcher opened the book and then boarded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:29.200
a domestic flight, spending the night in a hotel&nbsp;
in his hometown. Even miles away from the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:33.760
the researcher still experienced a&nbsp;
fantastical dream and during this dream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:39.760
he was approached by SCP-1230-1. The Book Keeper&nbsp;
was happy to explain the workings of the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:45.680
describing how SCP-1230 implanted a dream in the&nbsp;
reader’s subconscious mind from the moment they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:51.840
saw the phrase “A hero is born” on the page.
He claimed that he was the sole being with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:57.680
the ability to manipulate what he described as&nbsp;
“fantasy scapes”, and that he enjoyed doing this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:02.720
aiming to sculpt these fantastical dreams in such&nbsp;
a way that the dreamer experiencing them would get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:08.240
the most enjoyment out of being the hero of&nbsp;
their own story. Thanks to the Book Keeper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:12.960
the length of the dreams vary. They could&nbsp;
be quick, only lasting forty-five seconds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:17.920
or seem to last as long as two hundred years&nbsp;
from the dreamer’s point of view. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:22.880
in the real world, the person experiencing the&nbsp;
Book Keeper’s fantasy scape would only actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:28.800
be asleep for an average duration of time.
SCP-1230 only takes effect on living beings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:34.800
with the capacity for imagination. In other&nbsp;
words, only human beings are affected by the book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:40.080
and able to enter their own bespoke fantasy.&nbsp;
When the SCP Foundation attempted to use a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:46.160
mechanical arm to open SCP-1230, the pages&nbsp;
remained blank, leading the Book Keeper to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:52.080
explain to a subsequent dreamer that only beings&nbsp;
who can dream may find the phrase “A hero is born”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:58.080
within. Most animals or any machines that view&nbsp;
the book will not experience its effects at all.&nbsp;

00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:03.360
So, what can a person do within the fantasy&nbsp;
scapes of these dreams? Is it an open sandbox&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:08.720
to do anything and everything you can think of,&nbsp;
or do these fantastical realms have their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:14.160
rules? Well, for a start, they seem to be&nbsp;
completely safe spaces. During testing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:18.960
the Foundation conducted a number of experiments&nbsp;
that involved getting members of D Class personnel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:25.440
to read SCP-1230 and test the limitations of the&nbsp;
dream worlds the Book Keeper created for them.&nbsp;

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:30.560
Why use D Class personnel to test out&nbsp;
something seemingly as harmless as SCP-1230?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:35.760
Because the Foundation wanted to know if&nbsp;
a person could die in one of these dreams.&nbsp;

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:40.000
One test involved ordering a D Class to&nbsp;
immediately find a way to terminate himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:45.120
once he found himself inside the dream world.&nbsp;
He awakened after only forty-five seconds of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:50.960
being asleep, and described being at the peak of&nbsp;
a volcano called “The Ashen Spire.” Apparently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:55.760
within the dream, this test subject had been sent&nbsp;
on a quest to recover a sword known as “Caladius,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.760 --> 00:07:00.960
the Blessed Blade.” He said these names had&nbsp;
come to him as if he had known them all along.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:04.560
Following the Foundation’s orders,&nbsp;
the D Class attempted to throw himself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:09.600
into the volcano before him, only to wake&nbsp;
up moments later after feeling intense heat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:12.880
but suffering no adverse&nbsp;
effects in the real world.&nbsp;

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:18.320
During the following experiment, another D Class&nbsp;
was told to try and injure himself in the fantasy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:24.880
scape, but not a fatal injury. This subject awoke&nbsp;
after six hours of sleep, feeling a numbed pain in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:30.720
the same place he had attempted to harm himself,&nbsp;
but this sensation was never intense, in fact, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:35.440
was barely noticeable. In his dream, the D Class&nbsp;
said that he had conversed with the Book Keeper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:39.760
who had shown concern that the man was trying&nbsp;
to cause himself injury but thanked him for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:44.640
not terminating himself immediately like the&nbsp;
“other rude fellow” that had preceded him.&nbsp;

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:50.480
So, all of this sounds pretty great, right? A book&nbsp;
that lets someone live out a fantasy adventure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:55.920
in which they are the hero of their own bespoke&nbsp;
story. What could possibly be the downside? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.920 --> 00:08:01.680
the downside is this: What if someone&nbsp;
preferred their fantasy to the real world?&nbsp;

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:06.480
Now comes the unfortunate story of a&nbsp;
Professor working for the SCP Foundation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:12.000
a lifelong fan of tabletop roleplaying games like&nbsp;
Dungeons &amp; Dragons. The Professor filed a request&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:18.160
for access to SCP-1230, and it was granted. He&nbsp;
was apparently shaking with pure excitement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:23.120
After reading “A hero is born” in the book, he&nbsp;
didn’t even wait until later to go to sleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:27.200
instead opting to doze off right then&nbsp;
and there at the desk where he’d read it.&nbsp;

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:32.000
After the Professor had spent fifteen&nbsp;
entire hours asleep, concern had begun to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:37.600
spread among the other researchers. He remained&nbsp;
sleeping, living out his own fantasy adventure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:43.040
for a further nine hours, spending a total of&nbsp;
twenty four hours in the dream world that the Book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:48.640
Keeper had made for him. When the Professor awoke,&nbsp;
he appeared to look around the room confused,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:53.040
unsure of where he was when approached&nbsp;
by the Foundation’s security personnel.&nbsp;

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:56.320
Medical staff examined him&nbsp;
and explained where he was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.320 --> 00:09:01.600
after which the Professor seemed to regain&nbsp;
his memory. After being reminded who he was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:06.560
the Professor excused himself to the restroom.&nbsp;
He didn’t emerge, and fifteen minutes later a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:12.000
member of the medical team went to check on him.&nbsp;
Sadly, the Professor was dead. Scribbled on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:19.120
nearby bathroom wall was one simple phrase,&nbsp;
his last words: “I can’t go back to this.”&nbsp;

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:23.680
Attempting to immediately use SCP-1230&nbsp;
and make contact with the Book Keeper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:28.320
another doctor tried to find the “A hero is&nbsp;
born” phrase within the pages of the book.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:34.400
However, instead, the pages were soaking wet,&nbsp;
and on every single page, the same message:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:40.800
“I am so sorry. I never intended for this to&nbsp;
happen. I just wanted to make people happy.”&nbsp;

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:45.600
For three weeks the book stayed like this until&nbsp;
the same doctor placed a sticky note inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:50.320
SCP-1230 that said “I’d like to&nbsp;
talk to you, if that’s alright.”&nbsp;

00:09:50.320 --> 00:09:55.760
That night, the doctor fell asleep and dreamt&nbsp;
of being in a dark void. There was only one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.760 --> 00:10:00.880
source of light in the endless darkness, a&nbsp;
streetlamp, under which he found the Book Keeper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:06.960
sat in a puddle. The old bearded man was sobbing&nbsp;
uncontrollably, his cloak soaking wet with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:11.520
tears. He explained, through his overwhelming&nbsp;
sadness, what had happened to the Professor:&nbsp;

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:18.160
“He had such an active imagination! I was able to&nbsp;
create a vast and beautiful universe for him and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.160 --> 00:10:24.080
it was obvious that he had wanted a life like that&nbsp;
for so long. He conquered foul beasts and rescued&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.080 --> 00:10:31.120
princesses… He built kingdoms and even raised a&nbsp;
family… but he never wanted to leave. He delved so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.120 --> 00:10:37.040
far into his fantasy world that I soon realized&nbsp;
he preferred his dream over the real world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.040 --> 00:10:42.560
I reminded him that this was all merely an&nbsp;
illusion, but he wouldn’t listen to me. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:48.640
stated that if he was ever forced to leave,&nbsp;
he would immediately end his life. I tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.640 --> 00:10:52.880
to keep him happy for as long as I could…”
The Book Keeper revealed that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:58.800
from the Professor’s point of view, the fantasy&nbsp;
life he had lived lasted two hundred years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.800 --> 00:11:03.840
As much as the old man had tried to hold on and&nbsp;
keep the Professor happy for as long as possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:09.680
eventually, he had to release him from the fantasy&nbsp;
scape. And, as a result, the Professor had ended&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:16.080
his own life rather than return to reality.
SCP-1230 resumed its normal function a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:21.520
days later, after the doctor left another note&nbsp;
between the pages, offering some friendly advice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:26.960
to the Book Keeper. Now, it sits quietly on a&nbsp;
bookshelf in the Site 12 library, surrounded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:32.160
by fictional novels, the perfect material to&nbsp;
inspire more fantasy scapes in the future.&nbsp;

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:36.600
We all like to dream that we’re the heroes&nbsp;
of our own stories. But, to quote the Book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:43.040
Keeper himself, “As sweet as dreams may&nbsp;
be, eventually we all have to wake up.”&nbsp;

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:48.880
Now go check out “SCP-1762 - Where&nbsp;
The Dragons Went” and “SCP-348 - A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:52.880
Gift from Dad” for more anomalies that&nbsp;
are sure to tug at your heart strings.

